# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Seven Crates Against One Drone

**Story & Context:** The debut runs at 128 BPM. Mariam establishes one low arghul drone and a three-note melody figure. Owen opens in 7/8 grouped 3+2+2, then changes the same riff to 2+2+3 without altering its pitches. Youssef marks only the first accent of each subgroup on riq. Lina sustains the root across both grouping versions, making the metric rewrite feel more dramatic because the tonal center never moves. Ruth enters in the second cycle with one low fifth held across the barline. At the midpoint, the band jumps to 5/8 for four bars while Mariam continues the exact same drone. The final section returns to 7/8 but uses a third grouping, 2+3+2, proving that one bar length can carry three different physical identities.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Seven citrus crates arranged in uneven groups of three, two, and two beside one continuous painted line representing the drone, with arghul pipes and packing labels in shallow focus. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “seven crates,” “one line,” “count it again,” and “drone unchanged.”

## 2. Bay Nine Recounts the Same Bar

**Story & Context:** At 132 BPM, the track stays in 9/8 for nearly its entire duration but continually changes internal grouping: 3+3+3, 2+2+2+3, 4+2+3, then 2+3+4. Owen’s baritone riff retains the same pitch set while moving accents. Mariam repeats one four-note arghul cell over the fixed drone, refusing to mirror the guitar’s regrouping. Youssef becomes the principal meter guide during a drumless middle section. Lina alternates between long root sustains and clipped octave attacks. Ruth uses short tongue-slap figures on the weakest expected beats, deliberately destabilizing the listener’s first interpretation of the bar. The track never changes meter, yet it feels as if the floor plan keeps being redrawn.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Warehouse bay marker 9 above four different hand-drawn grouping diagrams for the same bar length, one uninterrupted drone line crossing all four. Spoken fragments limited to “same nine,” “new grouping,” “do not move the root,” and “count from another edge.”

## 3. The Roller Stops Between Eleven and Four

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 116 BPM and uses the EP’s widest metric contrast. It begins in 11/8 with a sparse prepared-guitar figure while Mariam sustains one drone and plays almost no melody. The ensemble then drops abruptly into 4/4 without changing tempo or root, making the common meter feel strangely oversized. Youssef uses only muted riq strokes in the 11/8 section and open jing accents in 4/4. Lina holds one fretless bass note through the exact transition point. Ruth plays a descending minor second only after the new meter is fully established. In the last passage, the group alternates one bar of 11/8 and one bar of 4/4 while the arghul drone remains continuous. The final stop cuts every instrument except the drone for one additional breath before Mariam ends naturally.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Old citrus conveyor roller frozen between lane markers 11 and 4, one straight copper-colored line continuing past both numbers, dry warehouse lighting and packing tags. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “eleven,” “four,” “roller stops,” and “air continues.”

